// and on Zenodo (concept DOI 10.5281/zenodo.22855036). This is the linguistic
// analysis that the ProtoBias audit grew into: it states the semantic distinction
// the benchmark design presupposes but does not itself separate. Honest status:
// it reports no new data, and says so. Do not describe it as an experiment, and do
// not claim the three conditions have been tested — the paper argues they must be
// held apart before a typicality effect can be interpreted, not that they have been.
const doi = "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22855036";
const lingbuzz = "https://lingbuzz.net/lingbuzz/010343";
---

<Base
  title="Typicality in referent choice · Xiaochuan Li"
  description="A description built on a superordinate category term does not pick out its members evenly. Three conditions have to be held apart before a typicality effect on referent choice says anything determinate. Working paper; no new results."
  showProgress={true}
  toc={true}
>
  <article class="prose">
    <p class="!mb-6 !text-sm"><a href="/research" class="!text-[var(--color-accent)]">← Current Work</a></p>
    <div class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-[var(--color-text-dim)] font-medium mb-2">
      Working paper
    </div>
    <h1>Typicality in referent choice</h1>
    <p class="text-[var(--color-text-muted)] italic !text-lg !mb-3">
      When a description built on a category term fits two candidates, how much of the choice is the
      description doing, and how much is typicality?
    </p>
    <p class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-dim)] !mb-8">
      <a href={lingbuzz}>lingbuzz/010343</a> · <a href={doi}>doi:10.5281/zenodo.22855036</a> ·
      CC&nbsp;BY&nbsp;4.0.
    </p>

    <p>
      Say <em>an animal perched on a branch</em>. The phrase is true of a great many things, but hearers
      do not treat its extension as flat: a bird comes to mind before a stick insect, a dog before an
      octopus. The question this paper takes up is not whether that readiness exists, but how much work
      it does in fixing reference, and what in the rest of the description displaces it.
    </p>

    <h2>Three conditions, not one</h2>
    <p>
      The question is usually posed loosely, as “does typicality affect which referent is chosen.” But a
      description built on a category term can meet two candidates in at least three different semantic
      situations. Hold the pair fixed — a monkey on a branch above a red leaf pile, a stick insect on a
      branch above a green one — and vary only the description:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Neither candidate is excluded.</strong> <em>An animal perched on a branch</em> is satisfied by both. Only here is typicality doing identifying work without a competing constraint.</li>
      <li><strong>The description picks out the atypical one.</strong> <em>…above a green leaf pile</em> is satisfied by the stick insect alone. Choosing the monkey now fails a description that one candidate alone meets.</li>
      <li><strong>The description picks out the typical one.</strong> <em>…above a red leaf pile</em> is satisfied by the monkey alone. Constraint and typicality agree, and the condition serves as a control.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The same choice response means something different in each. Collapsing the first two is easy, since
      the category term alone is true of both candidates in all three, and it turns “typicality influences
      reference” into a claim that cannot be assigned to a condition.
    </p>

    <h2>What a choice can and cannot report</h2>
    <p>
      Forced-choice referent selection is the right primary instrument here: it is the only readout that
      directly reports which candidate a description is taken to pick out. But it has limits that have to
      be stated rather than assumed away.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>A rejected candidate may have been rejected as false or as a worse fit, and the task cannot tell those apart. Judgment data stay necessary alongside it.</li>
      <li>There is an endpoint but no course: the measure says where interpretation landed, not how it got there.</li>
      <li>In picture-based versions the restricting detail has to be recovered from the image before it can override anything. A wrong answer is then ambiguous between “the description did not override typicality” and “the two pictures were not told apart on that dimension.” The paper proposes a per-item discriminability control, and is explicit that it is necessary but not sufficient.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Where it comes from</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href="/research/cross-lingual-protobias">cross-lingual ProtoBias</a> audit ran a picture-based
      design of exactly this shape at scale, and found a measurable effect. What it did not establish is how
      its items distribute across the three conditions — that would require checking, item by item, whether
      the full description holds of both candidates, not merely whether one dimension is mentioned. This
      paper is the linguistic analysis that the audit needed: it states the distinction the design
      presupposes, and sets the conditions under which a comparison would answer something.
    </p>
    <p>
      It also positions the work against its nearest neighbour. Kobrock, Uhlemann and Gotzner (2024) study
      comprehension of superordinate reference directly, and propose relaxing their contexts so that a
      level of specificity is sufficient but not necessary for identification. That is a different
      manipulation: there, reference stays unambiguous and what varies is how specific the speaker is;
      here, the full description is true of two competing candidates and the listener must still choose.
      The two can be combined, but neither subsumes the other.
    </p>

    <p>
      Read against the <a href="/research">current work on epistemic modality</a>, the two are the same
      event approached from different points. A description narrowing to one candidate and a possibility
      being revised when evidence arrives are both cases of a linguistic form settling what stays
      available. This paper is about where the narrowing lands; the modality work asks what happens in
      between.
    </p>

    <h2>Status</h2>
    <p>
      Published as a working paper, not peer-reviewed. It reports no new results, and the design
      requirements it states are written for future studies rather than as faults in existing ones. The
      literature search behind its positioning section was a title-and-abstract query, not an exhaustive
      review, and the paper says so.
    </p>

    <p class="!mt-8 text-sm">
      <a href={lingbuzz} class="!text-[var(--color-accent)]">Read on LingBuzz ↗</a>
      <span class="text-[var(--color-text-dim)]"> · </span>
      <a href={doi} class="!text-[var(--color-accent)]">Zenodo (DOI, PDF) ↗</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
